# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Configuration for fastcache-compile-node.service.
#
# TWO settings, and only two: --scheduler and --advertise. Both are genuinely
# site-specific and have no defensible default, which is why the unit reads them
# from a file rather than carrying them. The service will not start until they
# are set.
#
# The compilers are NOT among them. The worker surveys this machine at startup
# and serves what it finds, so the half of the configuration that used to be
# redone after every toolchain upgrade is gone.
#
# Arguments are passed through the shell-less systemd expansion, so quote
# nothing and keep each on one line.

# The scheduler's --listen-scheduler endpoint, and the endpoint CLIENTS should
# use to reach this worker. The second must be an address they can actually
# resolve -- the scheduler hands it to them verbatim, so a worker that advertises
# 127.0.0.1 is leased and then never answers.
#
#FASTCACHE_NODE_ARGS=--scheduler=build-cache.internal:6675 --advertise=worker-01.internal:6676

# Add --toolchain=<compiler> to serve a NARROWER set than this machine holds --
# a build farm pinned to a curated toolchain, say. Naming any pins the worker to
# exactly those, and a plain compiler path is what to give it: the worker
# computes the fingerprint itself, the same way its clients do. Repeat per
# toolchain.
#
# There is still no default COMPILER, only discovery of the ones installed: a
# default is how a job ends up running against something nobody chose.
#
#FASTCACHE_NODE_ARGS=... --toolchain=/usr/bin/g++ --toolchain=/usr/bin/clang++

# Add --no-toolchain-discovery to stop the survey. With no --toolchain alongside
# it the worker has nothing to serve and refuses to start, which is deliberate:
# it is the one shape that cannot work, and it is better refused than left
# registering and rejecting every job.

# Add --requirepass=<secret> when the scheduler was started with one. Prefer a
# drop-in with a tighter mode over putting a secret in this file:
#
#     systemctl edit fastcache-compile-node

# Add --admin-listen=<port> to serve /metrics and /healthz. It is off unless
# asked for, and a bare port binds loopback -- a scrape surface on a public
# interface is a decision, not something you get by typing a port. Write
# --admin-listen=0.0.0.0:6677 when you mean the network.
#
# Worth setting even if nothing scrapes it: without /healthz a supervisor can
# tell that this process is alive but not that it is answering, which is exactly
# the state a wedged worker is in.
